      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="1373738160">
          <groundspeak:date>2026-07-23T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="25790230">MaybeSunshine</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP403 Rimbey [FOUND]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Eighteen years after it was first logged, the most obvious approach to this cap remains available because the adjacent lot is still vacant. Perhaps Rimbey isn’t growing quite as fast as some other Alberta communities I could name. &lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="683276760">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-02T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2164408">indianajoan&amp;marcus</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP403-Rimbey

This one is right by a backyard fence. The dog was upset that I was there. TFTC&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="683292808">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-29T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="946600">Black Shark</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP403 Rimbey&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Off to the Ponoka Trade Fair via Rimbey!  It's more scenic past Sylvan Lake and Bentley than Hwy 2, you see.  In truth, I swung by to log the brass cap before the logging rules change.  Mama Shark was along for the trade fair but is good natured about being hijacked, rerouted and delayed when she travels with us!  She was forewarned this time that we would be going to Ponoka via Rimbey and back to Red Deer through Mirror and was still eager to come. Such a sweet muggle! &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our drive had started in sunshine, moved to sunshine on a rain misted windshield and progressed to wipers on steady slow to keep the now darkly cloudy sky, not to mention the road, in clear view.  As we neared Rimbey we noticed that oncoming traffic didn't have their wipers on and the road was drier; by the time we got here the sun had peeked out.  It shone into the easily located hole that's home to this battered cap and glistened on the rain on its surface.  A few errant blades of grass to move out of the way and a wipe of the cap to diminish the watery shine and photographic proof of the find was recorded.  Thanks for the cap, outforthehunt, which showed how much Rimbey has grown since it was more familiar.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="675767149">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="52.647000" lon="-114.229700" />
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="314197">EdgeMonks</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP403 Rimbey, 918m&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I made the mistake of driving down the very muddy alley to approach this cap. With all the rain last night, it would have been a much cleaner approach to park on the street in front of the cap and walk on the grass. I moved an empty water bottle and read the numbers - nice and quick!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="670526527">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-03-11T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="181446">DanOCan</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP403:  Rimbey&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Having read the previous logs and having looked at a lot of the context photos, I thought I had a good chance of finding this one, even with all the snow.  Sure enough, once I got the courage to walk up to ground zero right at someone's back fence, I only needed to do a little snow moving before revealing the depression containing the cap.  Digging in with my bare hands (why did I forget to bring gloves on this trip????) soon revealed the brass enough to get my photographic proof of visit.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
      <groundspeak:travelbugs />
